------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 RIPKit for PCBoard
==============================================================================




Requirements
---------------------------------------

   * PCBoard v15.2
   * A fair amount of experience with a RIPaint type of program and PCBoard
   * Plenty of time (you will now have more screens to update for your system).


The Goal Of RIPKit
---------------------------------------

   The goal of RIPKit is to simply provide PCBoard SysOps with a STARTING
   POINT (capitalized for emphasis) for "RIPping" their systems.  By no
   means is this kit designed to be a hands-off / self-maintaining RIPscrip
   add-in for PCBoard.

   If you keep the focus of this product in mind, you'll understand the reason
   we listed experience with a paint program and PCBoard as a requirement.
   Without this experience, you'll quickly get lost in the complexity of a
   heavily modified system for RIPscrip.


Support For RIPKit
---------------------------------------

   RIPKit was put together by the technical support staff of CDC in some
   of their free time between answering calls and messages.  As such, support
   for the product is extremely limited.

   Support for RIPkit can *only* be obtained via the Salt Air BBS.  Customers
   asking questions via other means (e.g., fax or phone), will be referred
   to the Salt Air BBS to ask their questions.


Installation Instructions
---------------------------------------

1) Change to your \PCB directory.  Make a RIP directory by typing "MD RIP"
   at the DOS prompt.  Change to the newly created RIP subdirectory and
   unzip RIPKIT.ZIP using the following command:

       PKUNZIP RIPKIT.ZIP -d

   This will create the following new subdirectories:

       \PCB\RIP\        - Where file replacements for PCBTEXT records are
       \PCB\RIP\PPE\    - PPEs (and associated files) used in the RIPKit.
       \PCB\RIP\MENUS\  - MNUs (and associated files) used in the RIPKit.
       \PCB\RIP\DOCS\   - Documentation for RIPKit (Q&A, Common Concerns)

   NOTE:  If your system involves a network, you only have to have one copy
          of the RIPKit files.  If performance is a concern, store the RIPKit
          files on each workstation of your network.

2) Add the following line to AUTOEXEC.BAT (remember to do this to each
   machine in your system).

       SET PCBRIP=C:\PCB\RIP

   If your files are not in C:\PCB\RIP\, enter the appropriate directory.
   For example, Salt Air, stores all of the RIPKit files on the server in
   the H:\PCB\RIP\ subdirectory.  Consequently, our line in AUTOEXEC.BAT
   looks like this:

       SET PCBRIP=H:\PCB\RIP

   NOTE:  Do *NOT* add the trailing backslash to this environment variable.
          If you do, the RIPKit stuff will not work.  Throughout the
          PCBTEXT.RIP (and several display files), relative references
          are made.  Example:

             %%PCBRIP%\DISPLAYIT

          When PCBoard substitutes the environent variable, the result is:

             %C:\PCB\RIP\DISPLAYIT

          Obviously, if you have the trailing backslash, the resulting
          substutition is:

             %C:\PCB\RIP\\DISPLAYIT

          Since this is not a valid filename specification, it won't work.

3) Move PCBTEXT.RIP from the \PCB\RIP subdirectory to your PCBTEXT
   subdirectory.  If your system has a local copy of PCBTEXT for each node
   or workstation, take that into consideration.

4) Move WELCOME.RIP from the \PCB\RIP subdirectory to the directory where your
   WELCOME file is.  If your system has a local copy of the WELCOME file for
   each node/workstation, keep that in mind.

   NOTE:  If you have changed the name of your WELCOME file to something else,
          you will have to rename WELCOME.RIP to the name of your welcome file and
          add a .RIP extension.  For example, if PCBSetup | File Locations | Display
          Files shows:

             Name/Loc of WELCOME File          : C:\PCB\GEN\WELC

          Rename your WELCOME.RIP to WELC.RIP after copying it to C:\PCB\GEN\.

5) The next group of files relate to conferences.  The configuration for each
   conference specifies where the User Menu (BRDM), SysOp Menu (BRDS), and
   NEWS file are stored.

   Move/Copy BRDM.RIP and BRDS.RIP to the same location as your BRDM and BRDS
   files.  If you have changed the names of your BRDM and BRDS files, don't
   forget to change the names of the .RIP counterparts.  For example, if your
   Main Board Configuration screen shows:

        Name/Loc of User's Menu     : C:\PCB\GEN\USERS
        Name/Loc of Sysop's Menu    : C:\PCB\GEN\SYSOP

   Rename BRDM.RIP to USERS.RIP and BRDS.RIP to SYSOP.RIP after copying/moving
   them to the appropriate subdirectory.

   Do the same thing for the NEWS.RIP file making sure it gets copied to the
   right directory and the name of the corresponding .RIP file is correct.
   Once you have taken care of the placement and naming of the NEWS.RIP file,
   edit the file with a text editor because it hard codes the name of your
   actual NEWS file.

   What happens with the RIPKit is PCBoard finds the NEWS.RIP, displays the
   NEWS_.RIP located in the \PCB\RIP\ subdirectory (to setup the display
   for RIP, the text window, etc.).  Finally, your normal NEWS file is
   displayed.

6) Unzip the CMDFILES.ZIP (located in \PCB\RIP) into the directory specified by
   the following field in PCBSetup | File Locations System Files:

        Location of Command Display Files : C:\PCB\CMDFILES\

   Using this example, you would type:

        PKUNZIP \PCB\RIP\CMDFILES.ZIP C:\PCB\CMDFILES

   NOTE:  If you do not have a directory specified in PCBSetup, enter one
          (like \PCB\CMDFILES) and create the directory if necessary.

7) Update PCBML.DAT with RIP Language (Check all nodes to make sure they are
   configured to run multiple languages, and that they all point to the same
   PCBML.DAT.  Example PCBML.DAT:

              Language             Extension  Country  CodePage  Yes  No
        อออออออออออออออออออออออออ  อออออออออ  อออออออ  ออออออออ  อออ  ออ
        1 - Salt Air (Customized)                                 Y    N
        2 - Automated (Standard)      STD                         Y    N
        3 - Sub-menus                 SUB                         Y    N
        4 - RIPscrip                  RIP                         Y    N

   NOTE:  For these language changes to have any effect, you MUST have
          enabled multi-lingual support in PCBSetup | Configuration
          Options | System Control. Without that option enabled, callers
          will not be presented with the available list of languages and
          therefore will never get a chance to go into RIPscrip.

8) Unzip FLAG32R.ZIP in the \PCB\RIP subdirectory to your \PCB\FLAG
   subdirectory (unless you've already installed FLAG.PPE).  Once this
   is done, modify record 549 in your *PCBTEXT.RIP* to look like the
   following:

    ีออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออ
    ณ   Record No.:  549          Record Length:  60        Justi
    รฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤ
    ณ(@TIMELEFT@ min left), (H)elp, (V)iew, (F)lag, More
    ณ!C:\PCB\FLAG\FLAG.PPE
    ิออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออ

   NOTE:  You may already have David Terry's FLAG.PPE installed on your
          system.  If you do, simply update your PCBTEXT.RIP (record
          549) with the appropriate information.

9) Modify record 387 in the default PCBTEXT file.  This record is the
   "Enter Language # to use" prompt.  You want this record to read:

    ีออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออ
    ณ   Record No.:  387          Record Length:  56
    รฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤฤ
    ณEnter Language # to use (Enter)=no change
    ณ!%PCBRIP%\PPE\LANGPRMP.PPE
    ิออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออออ
    
   The LANGPRMP.PPE auto-detects if the user has RIP capabilities.  If
   so, it automatically picks the language number which is defined as
   having a .RIP extension in PCBML.DAT.

10) Read CONCERN.DOC included with the RIPKit.  It contains valuable
    information regarding adding RIPKit to *YOUR* system.

